// PARAM: --analysis interval --context entry-state
// Recursive factorial under entry-state context sensitivity. Solver-key
// inspection (not just this CLI report) confirms context separation itself
// is exact: factorial's entry is covered by four distinct, precise
// contexts (n=[1,1], [2,2], [3,3], [4,4]), each with an exact n -- so this
// is NOT a #108/context-sensitivity imprecision, and NOT recursive
// contexts collapsing or widening at the key level.
//
// The actual mechanism: at each n>=2 context, the "return 1" base-case
// edge (guarded by n<2) is correctly proved unreachable there (is_bot on
// that branch's own witness variable, n, is true) -- but the edge's own
// #ret := 1 assignment transfer does not itself check that its source
// state is already witness-bottom before computing, so it still produces
// a non-bottom #ret=[1,1] from a provably-dead predecessor. FunctionResult
// then legitimately joins that spurious [1,1] with the live branch's exact
// result, widening every factorial(n>=2) result to [1,n!] instead of
// [n!,n!]. Reproduces identically under plain --analysis interval with no
// --context at all (minimal case: `void f(n) { if (n<2) { return 1 } else
// { return n*100 } }`, f(5) -> [1,500] instead of [500,500]) -- a
// pre-existing, general non-relational-domain transfer-strictness gap
// (abstract transfers don't preserve "predecessor state is bottom" through
// an assignment), not specific to recursion, calls, or context
// sensitivity. Tracked as #113, independent of this group; once fixed,
// the two UNKNOWN checks below should become PROVED and this fixture
// should move out of known-imprecision/.
void factorial(n) {
  __voblint_check(0 < n); // PROVED

  if (n < 2) {
    return 1
  } else {
    r := factorial(n - 1);
    __voblint_check(0 < r); // PROVED
    return n * r
  }
}

void main() {
  a := factorial(3);
  b := factorial(4);

  __voblint_check(a == 6);   // UNKNOWN
  __voblint_check(b == 24)   // UNKNOWN
}
